window.GUIDE_CONTENT=Object.assign(window.GUIDE_CONTENT||{},{
  "memory-context": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"problem\">Why long chats drift</h2>\n<p>Long roleplays eventually exceed the amount of recent context a model can actively use. The bot can still have a character card and memory tools, but the conversation itself keeps growing until old scene details are no longer in the active window.</p>\n<p>The community guides describe the result as “amnesia”: forgotten injuries, equipment, promises, locations, NPC motives or character details that were obvious 100 messages ago.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"two-layers\">Separate permanent facts from current scene state</h2>\n<p>Bluhdwulf's guide uses two layers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pinned memory:</strong> compact facts that remain true for a while — core NPC traits, current equipment, relationship state, important world rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Scene snapshot / OOC reminder:</strong> short current-state information — where everyone is, what just happened, current injuries, active goals and unresolved threats.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This distinction is useful even if you do not use the exact same tools.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"compression\">Compress memory cards</h2>\n<p>The source proposes a compact symbol-heavy syntax to fit more into a 250-character memory card. You do not have to copy every symbol, but the principle is good: remove grammar that adds no meaning.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>[ROXY|Age:~20|@Workshop|Voice:Fast|Persona:Chaotic|Dynamic:Loyal to {{user}}]</pre></div>\n<p>Compare that to three sentences saying the same thing. Compact memory is easier to update and leaves more room for other state.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"current\">Store what is true now</h2>\n<p>One of the strongest rules in the guide is “current state only.” If the party left the workshop, change the location. If an item was used, remove it. If trust changed, update the same relationship entry instead of adding a new history card.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>update existing memory instead of endlessly appending;</li>\n<li>remove facts that are no longer relevant;</li>\n<li>keep exact names consistent;</li>\n<li>do not turn pinned memory into a transcript.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"snapshot\">Use a scene snapshot when the bot starts losing the thread</h2>\n<p>The “Story Compiler” idea is basically a structured recap generated outside the chat and pasted back in as an OOC state line. A useful snapshot can track:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>time / deadlines;</li>\n<li>current objective;</li>\n<li>location and spatial relationships;</li>\n<li>health/injuries;</li>\n<li>inventory and active equipment;</li>\n<li>important NPC intentions;</li>\n<li>the exact handoff state at the end of the last turn.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You do not need to refresh it every turn. The source suggests doing it every few dozen messages or when drift becomes obvious.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"tokens\">Tokens: the simple model</h2>\n<p>The layman's memory thread frames tokens as the finite “working space” the model uses for instructions, character information, recent conversation, memories and the next response. The exact tokenization is more complicated than “every symbol = one token,” so treat any simple character/token conversion as an approximation.</p>\n<p>The practical rule is still useful: every permanent instruction and every recalled block consumes room that could otherwise be used for recent conversation.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"limits\">What this cannot fix</h2>\n<p>No compression format can make a model remember infinite history. A command such as “never forget anything” does not increase the context window. These techniques work by keeping the most important state near the model instead of expecting it to recover everything from hundreds of old messages.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"workflow\">Practical long-RP workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Keep character identity/mechanics compact in the bot.</li>\n<li>Use memory for stable/current facts, not full history.</li>\n<li>Keep Lorebooks conditional so world data appears only when relevant.</li>\n<li>When a scene becomes complex, create a short state recap.</li>\n<li>Update/remove stale memory instead of only adding more.</li>\n<li>If the chat becomes irreparably confused, a clean continuation with a strong recap can be better than stacking more corrective prompts.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"visibility\">Model context is not the same as character knowledge</h2>\n<p>Hunter's updated Lorebook production guide separates four things that can otherwise get mixed together:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Model-visible:</strong> text currently available to the LLM.</li>\n<li><strong>Character-known:</strong> what this particular person has actually witnessed, learned or can legitimately know.</li>\n<li><strong>Player-known in fiction:</strong> information {{user}} has actually discovered in the story.</li>\n<li><strong>Artifact-visible:</strong> information somebody could read directly in the exported Lorebook or document.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A secret arriving in the model's context does not mean the character witnessed it, and a clue readable in an exported file is not necessarily revealed in-story. Specify who knows a secret and what would reveal it, especially in mystery/world bots.</p>\n<p>Keyword routing can reduce <em>early exposure</em>, but it cannot make a downloadable Lorebook file truly spoiler-proof.</p>\n<p><small>Additional source: Hunter, <em>Unified Lorebook LLM Production System v6</em> (original preserved separately).</small></p>\n<h2 id=\"official-semantic-memory\">The rebuilt SpicyChat Memory system — official November 2026 guide</h2>\n<p>SpicyChat has replaced the older Semantic Memory 2.0 instructions with a new <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/premium-features/memory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Memory guide</a>. <strong>Automatic Memory</strong> is documented for True Supporter and I'm All In subscribers in both new and existing chats; the separate <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/premium-features/memory-manager\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Memory Manager</a> is available to all paid tiers.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Before the chat exceeds context:</strong> no automatic memories are generated. The Manager may say \"Not creating memories yet\".</li>\n<li><strong>After context is exceeded:</strong> roughly every <strong>15 back-and-forths</strong>, automatic memory adds new facts, updates changed facts and removes outdated automatic facts.</li>\n<li><strong>One fact per memory:</strong> the memory is short and tagged with the character/persona/person it describes. Only relevant memories are retrieved for each reply.</li>\n<li><strong>Manually added and pinned memories:</strong> automatic updates do not delete them. Pinning also ensures they are included in model prompts, including before automatic memory starts.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Important nuance:</strong> before automatic memory starts, <strong>only pinned memories are sent to the character</strong>. A manual memory can be saved yet show under \"Not sent to the chatbot yet\". Pin a critical early fact if you need it actively supplied rather than assuming a saved, unpinned fact is already being used.</p>\n<p>Use the chat-message <strong>⋮ → Save to memories</strong> option to extract facts from a moment, inspect/untick or reword them, then save. In the Manager you can search, filter by person, pin, edit, delete, rate automatic memories, bulk-delete, and turn further memory creation off for that chat. Manually created memories have a <strong>250-character</strong> limit.</p>\n<p>Editing or deleting a chat message does <strong>not</strong> automatically correct a saved memory. After a retcon, review and edit/delete that memory separately. Earlier documentation that said memories begin after 8–10 messages and process in four-message batches is historical; do not use it for the rebuilt system.</p>\n<h2 id=\"retcons-and-source-truth\">Memory should preserve established truth, not speculation</h2>\n<p>When new events alter a relationship, injury, location or agreement, distinguish the latest confirmed state from an older summary. Memories and Lorebooks can be incomplete or stale; re-check them after a major revelation, timeline change or corrected misunderstanding instead of repeating obsolete facts in increasingly forceful instructions.</p>\n<p>Private thoughts, inferred feelings and disputed backstory are not automatically facts known by every character. A memory can preserve that something was <em>believed</em> without treating the belief as true. Maintain who observed, learned or confirmed the information.</p>\n<p>Compressed summaries should prioritize consequential facts and outstanding obligations rather than exhaustive transcripts. Test that the memory remains accurate after the user declines an expected action or chooses an alternate route.</p>\n<h2 id=\"memory-manager-nov3\">November 3 announcement versus current official documentation</h2>\n<p>A November 3 staff reply described a rollout that starts saving automatic memories near the context cutoff. That behavior is now explicitly documented in the official <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/premium-features/memory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Memory</a> and <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/premium-features/memory-manager\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Memory Manager</a> guides. Use those current instructions rather than treating the new design as an unconfirmed proposal.</p>\n<h3>Test without contaminating the story</h3>\n<ol><li>Record your account tier, model and active context size before comparing a fresh chat to an older one.</li><li>Watch the Manager status. No automatic entries while the conversation fits is expected.</li><li>After the chat exceeds context, allow roughly 15 exchanges between automatic memory updates, not 15 from the first message.</li><li>Check if the relevant facts are tagged to the right character and if changed facts replace obsolete ones.</li><li>If a crucial user-added memory is under \"Not sent to the chatbot yet\", pin it when appropriate.</li><li>After a story correction, edit or delete an inaccurate memory; messages and memories are stored separately.</li></ol>\n<p>Automatic memories improve continuity but do not guarantee perfect recall or override every contradictory permanent instruction. Keep the original character design compatible with possible development.</p>\n<h2 id=\"nov18-context\">context increased again on november 18</h2>\n<p>SpicyChat's November 18 announcement says <strong>Get A Taste now has 6K context</strong> and <strong>True Supporter has 12K</strong>. these are tier-level updates, not a promise that every model uses the full amount: the model's own context ceiling and the character setup still matter.</p>\n<p>the <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/advanced/tokens-and-context\">official Tokens &amp; Context page</a> was still showing <strong>4,096</strong> and <strong>8,192</strong> for those tiers at this check. those figures are older than the November 18 release announcement, so don't copy them into a new guide as current limits. Free and I'm All In changes were <strong>not</strong> announced in that post.</p>\n<p>more context helps keep more recent story visible, but it doesn't make memories unlimited or guarantee a bot will remember a detail correctly. the rebuilt Memory system still handles older facts in its own way.</p>\n<p><small>November 18 official Discord release announcement; checked against <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/advanced/tokens-and-context\">Tokens &amp; Context</a>.</small></p>\n<h2 id=\"josh-whiteboard-nov18\">the whiteboard example, without the confusing parts</h2>\n<p>Josh's whiteboard comparison is pretty handy: the model only gets the information that fits into the current request, along with recent messages and whatever other context SpicyChat includes. when the window fills up, old messages can fall out.</p>\n<p>the model isn't secretly keeping a complete personal memory of every earlier turn. pinned memories, automatic Memory, Lorebooks, Persona and Rulebook state can supply some information again, but they're different mechanisms.</p>\n<p>the exact <em>order</em> of Scenario, Personality, Persona, pinned memories and triggered Lorebooks in SpicyChat's constructed prompt isn't documented as a permanent public contract. don't repeat the thread's rough ordering as something that's always true. it's also too strong to say that every Greeting <em>must</em> be forgotten: a Greeting in older chat history may leave active context, but what remains available depends on what the app sends for that reply.</p>\n<p>more context generally keeps more of the recent story accessible; it doesn't guarantee attention or perfect continuity. a source being present in the prompt doesn't mean the model will use it correctly, and it doesn't prove the character has learned the information in-fiction.</p>"
  },
  "markdown-huds": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"markdown\">Visible Markdown tricks</h2>\n<p>Bluhdwulf's guide collects a few formatting patterns for RPG/system bots. Some are simple Markdown presentation tricks; others rely on how SpicyChat currently renders raw text, so they should be tested before depending on them.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"callouts\">Inline-code callouts</h2>\n<p>The guide uses single backticks for bright system-style callouts:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>`[SYSTEM NOTICE]`\n`• Type /status to inspect your character.`</pre></div>\n<p>This is useful for short UI-like notices, not paragraphs of normal narration.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"headings\">Compact headings / terminal flavor</h2>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>*SYSTEM INITIALIZING...*\n*CORE ENGINE: ONLINE.*\n\n**Welcome to the Living RPG Engine.**\n-</pre></div>\n<p>The visual effect depends on the current renderer, so keep the actual guide logic understandable even if formatting changes later.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"commands\">Fake “slash commands” as RP controls</h2>\n<p>You can teach the bot that user inputs such as <code>/menu</code>, <code>/status</code> or <code>/help</code> should be interpreted as special requests. These are not necessarily platform-native commands; they are conventions your bot can be instructed to recognize.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>When {{user}} types /status:\nshow the current character sheet and active objectives,\nthen return to normal roleplay.</pre></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"hidden\">Hidden HTML-comment state</h2>\n<p>The most interesting trick is using an HTML comment as a hidden state block:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>&lt;!-- [STATE: LOC:Town | HP:100 | GOLD:50 | GEAR:IronSword] --&gt;</pre></div>\n<p>The frontend may hide the comment visually while the raw text can still be part of what the model sees. That makes it possible to keep a compact state line in each response without filling the visible story with a giant HUD.</p>\n<div class=\"wip-banner\"><strong>Needs verification:</strong> this is exactly the kind of frontend/model behavior that can change. The draft keeps the technique because the source guide is recent, but it should be tested on the live site before this guide is marked complete.</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"anchor\">Seed the pattern in the greeting</h2>\n<p>If you want the model to maintain a state tag, put the initial state in the greeting and explicitly tell the bot to update it. Models are much more likely to continue a format already present in the conversation than invent it reliably later.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"short\">Keep state short</h2>\n<p>A hidden tracker still consumes output/context. Track only what matters:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>LOC:Town\nHP:82\nGOLD:35\nQUEST:Find Mira\nGEAR:Sword,Key\nREL:Mira=Suspicious</pre></div>\n<p>Do not turn the hidden block into a second story paragraph.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"failure\">Failure modes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>the model forgets to output the tag;</li>\n<li>the renderer exposes it visibly;</li>\n<li>the tag grows until it consumes too much reply space;</li>\n<li>the model invents/changes stats instead of applying your rules;</li>\n<li>voice/TTS or other features may read raw hidden text.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For anything important, test the current SpicyChat frontend/model and have a visible fallback such as <code>/status</code>.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"starter\">Minimal starter pattern</h2>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Personality rule:\nMaintain the STATE comment at the end of every response.\nUpdate only values changed by story events.\n\nGreeting:\n...opening scene...\n\n&lt;!-- [STATE: LOC:StartingVillage | HP:100 | GOLD:50 | QUEST:None] --&gt;</pre></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"native-appearance-2026\">Native Chat Appearance changes presentation</h2>\n<p>Since November 13, 2026, SpicyChat has a built-in <a href=\"/bots/chat-appearance/\">Chat Appearance</a> editor. It can set different colors for <code>*actions*</code>, quoted speech, <code>**bold**</code>, backtick highlights and OOC/command text, and it offers a Book or Script layout. These are <strong>display options</strong>, not guaranteed ways to change how an LLM interprets markdown or hidden HUD text.</p>\n<p>If a visual markdown trick stops looking right, test the Default theme versus Book/Script layouts, and then temporarily disable overlapping extension appearance controls. Hiding OOC in native appearance still sends the original command text to the model. For a true interactive counter with changing values, Rulebooks are more reliable than a decorative text-only HUD.</p>"
  },
  "rpg-bots": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"scope\">Decide whether you are building a story bot or a game engine</h2>\n<p>The RPG discussions show a common failure: creators try to make the LLM obey a tabletop ruleset with exact stats, costs, cooldowns, loot tables and combat math, then discover that the model treats some of it as inspiration instead of deterministic code.</p>\n<p>Before writing anything, decide which end you are closer to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Narrative RPG:</strong> stats and rules justify story outcomes, but the model can improvise.</li>\n<li><strong>System-heavy RPG:</strong> established numbers/rules should remain consistent and the bot must track state more carefully.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"fields\">Put the right information in the right place</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Personality:</strong> narrator behavior, how NPCs are created, how turns are framed, persistent game rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Scenario:</strong> the world's premise and current campaign setup.</li>\n<li><strong>Greeting:</strong> onboarding / character creation / first scene.</li>\n<li><strong>Lorebook:</strong> locations, factions, classes, magic, monsters, items and rules that only matter when triggered.</li>\n<li><strong>State/memory:</strong> current HP/resources/inventory/quests and already-established facts.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"onboarding\">Make the first turn playable</h2>\n<p>A good RPG greeting should explain just enough for the user to act. If character creation is required, ask for only the fields the bot truly needs.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Choose:\n1. Name\n2. Role / class\n3. One starting item\n\nThen begin in the world immediately.</pre></div>\n<p>Do not make the first message a 2,000-word rulebook unless reading the rulebook is actually the experience you want.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"npc\">Teach the bot to create side characters</h2>\n<p>For open-world RPG bots, define a compact NPC-generation rule instead of pre-writing 40 disposable characters:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>When a new NPC is needed:\ngive them a name, role, 2-3 distinctive traits,\na short goal and one relationship to the current scene.\nKeep established NPC details consistent when they return.</pre></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"rules\">Hard rules need persistent visibility</h2>\n<p>The LitRPG discussion reports that a bot with many Lorebook entries would remember only parts of a custom system and then invent costs/rules. The useful takeaway is that <strong>core rules that must always apply should not depend on a rare trigger</strong>.</p>\n<p>Put the small set of non-negotiable rules in a persistent field/state. Use Lorebooks for the detailed class/spell/item entries that only matter when referenced.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"state\">Once invented, established facts should stay canon</h2>\n<p>If you let the model invent classes or abilities, add a continuity rule:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Once a class, stat, ability, resource cost or world rule is established,\ntreat it as canon until progression or a story event explicitly changes it.</pre></div>\n<p>This gives the model freedom to create without re-rolling the system every five replies.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"simple\">Simplify before adding more mechanics</h2>\n<p>The LitRPG creator eventually reached the same conclusion many system-bot creators do: if 19 entries are being half-followed, adding entry 20 is unlikely to solve it. Reduce the always-on rules to the smallest viable system and let the Lorebook provide detail when relevant.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"test\">Test with deliberately different characters</h2>\n<p>A system that works for one Persona can fail for another. Test at least:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a straightforward “expected” player build;</li>\n<li>a weird build that stresses the rules;</li>\n<li>combat/resource use;</li>\n<li>leaving the intended quest path;</li>\n<li>returning to an old NPC/location after many messages.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"game-loop\">The basic TTRPG loop</h2>\n<p>Hunter's <em>TTRPG LLM Guide v2</em> explains the core loop as <strong>Describe → Invite → Resolve → Change</strong>. The world establishes what is happening, gives the player room to act, resolves what they attempt, then updates the situation so their choice matters.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Describe: The bridge is damaged and guards are approaching.\nInvite: Let the player decide whether to cross, hide or improvise.\nResolve: Judge their chosen action using the current rules.\nChange: Update the bridge, guards, injuries and future options.</pre></div>\n<p>Listed options should be suggestions, never the only permitted actions. A text RPG still needs to respond meaningfully when the player tries something unexpected.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"complexity-choice\">Choose the complexity before inventing stats</h2>\n<p>Hunter separates <strong>Story mode</strong>, <strong>Simple RPG mode</strong> and <strong>Crunchy mode</strong>. Story mode can resolve most situations narratively; simple mode uses a compact stat/check system; crunchy mode adds more explicit mechanics. Don't build a full rule engine just because the word “RPG” is in the premise.</p>\n<p>The four sample stats in Hunter's default simple system are Might, Agility, Mind and Presence. That's an example architecture, not a SpicyChat requirement. A custom setting may need fewer or different stats.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"state-classes\">Track different kinds of information separately</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Core state:</strong> things the player routinely needs (health, major resources, identity).</li>\n<li><strong>Active state:</strong> current position, injuries, effects, inventory and quest progress.</li>\n<li><strong>World flags:</strong> persistent consequences such as an opened gate, a defeated boss or a promised favor.</li>\n<li><strong>Reference data:</strong> stable world definitions and rules that belong in the card or Lorebook, not constantly changing state.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The useful rule is <strong>track only the state that can change play</strong>. A hundred visible statistics are not helpful if the model never uses them consistently.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"dice-choice\">Decide who rolls, and when</h2>\n<p>Hunter describes player-rolled, narrative-roll and external-dice approaches. Whichever you choose, make the source of randomness clear. Roll for genuinely uncertain meaningful actions, not trivial actions or things the world has already established as impossible.</p>\n<p>For example, opening an unlocked door usually needs no roll. Jumping between two moving trains might. If the character cannot physically lift a mountain in the setting, a lucky roll should not quietly invent that ability.</p>\n<p><small>Additional source: Hunter, <em>TTRPG LLM Guide v2</em> (original preserved separately).</small></p>\n<h2 id=\"player-agency-update\">Apply the player-agency rule during resolution</h2>\n<p>Hunter's TTRPG v2 emphasizes a correction to the Scenario rules: the world resolves the action the player <em>attempted</em>; it does not write the player's next voluntary choice, reaction or dialogue. NPCs, hazards and enemies may affect the player's body and surroundings, while the response returns control once the new state is clear.</p>\n<p>The recommended four-stat and 1d6 examples in that LLM guide are optional <em>design defaults</em>, not native SpicyChat mechanics. Choose an actual resolution method before promising rolls or state updates, and distinguish simulated rolls from externally generated random results.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"open-agency-rpg\">Resolve attempts without taking over the player</h2>\n<p>Establish what an action attempts, the relevant obstacles and the world's response. Failure or opposition can impose consequences, but the model should not invent the user's voluntary follow-up, internal reaction or consent. Leave meaningful decisions open after resolving the currently established event.</p>\n<p>Do not make every conflict follow a predetermined winning or losing sequence. Durable world rules should govern the response to new circumstances, while the character's judgment, allies and opponents retain independent motives and incomplete knowledge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"canon-and-state-rpg\">Separate canon, current state and speculative possibilities</h2>\n<p>Persistent information includes species rules, setting constraints and a character's abilities. Ongoing state includes location, injuries, inventory, alliances, discovered clues and current objectives. Keep the layers consistent while recognizing that the character may not know every rule or secret the creator has written.</p>\n<p>When a state changes through play, update the appropriate record or confirmed history rather than pretending older Lorebook entries automatically understand what happened. Test that the system handles a route the author did not plan while still respecting the setting's rules.</p>"
  },
  "tagging": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"why\">tags are there to tell people what they're opening</h2>\n<p>tags help people find bots, but they're also how someone decides whether a story sounds interesting or whether they'd rather avoid it. i'd rather have a few accurate tags than a full list that doesn't really match the plot.</p>\n<p>SpicyChat currently documents up to <strong>12 tags</strong>, with at least <strong>2</strong> required for Public bots. it suggests choosing a few that really describe the character.</p>\n<h2 id=\"examples\">a couple of examples</h2>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Bot: a suspicious guard investigating a missing key.\nUseful themes: mystery, distrust, modern setting.\nNot helpful: adding unrelated tags for extra views.\n\nBot: two colleagues whose relationship might develop.\nBe clear that romance is optional, not a fixed outcome.</pre></div>\n<p>those are examples of themes, not a claim that every phrase is an actual tag in the editor. use the live picker. our <a href=\"/tags.json\">tags.json</a> is a dated snapshot.</p>\n<h2 id=\"implied\">when the actual premise isn't tagged</h2>\n<p>sometimes a bot doesn't have the tag you're filtering out, but the title or Greeting still describes that theme. for example, a story could involve a partner secretly starting another romantic relationship without being tagged Cheating or NTR. even if the creator calls it \"complicated feelings\", that's still something people might want to know before opening it.</p>\n<p>i'd look at the <strong>actual premise</strong>, not just the labels. but don't go too far the other way either: a possible optional story branch isn't proof that something has definitely happened.</p>\n<h2 id=\"description\">sometimes a short description helps more</h2>\n<p>the tag picker can't cover every possible story. if the whole premise depends on betrayal, a fixed user role or some other important limitation, a clear title or description helps. you don't need to spoil every twist, just don't hide the main hook behind unrelated tags.</p>\n<h2 id=\"community\">community labels and QoL Implied Themes</h2>\n<p>DragonScript QoL has been discussing an <strong>Implied Themes</strong> feature and possible community labels from the Archive. these would be third-party hints, <em>not</em> official SpicyChat tags or confirmed facts just because somebody submitted them.</p>\n<p>a useful system would need evidence from the public title, Greeting or visible definition, separate confidence levels, ways to dispute mistakes and some moderation. don't automatically treat a single report as proof.</p>\n<h2 id=\"filters\">what do filters actually catch?</h2>\n<p>SpicyChat's tag filters can include or exclude the tags a creator selected. they can't reliably catch a theme that was never tagged. QoL's word and custom filters can help in other ways, but they also won't always understand the story correctly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"last\">one quick check before publishing</h2>\n<p>read your title, tags and Greeting as if you're a new visitor. do they describe the same bot? would someone filtering for these tags actually find what they were expecting?</p>\n<p><small>source: <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/creating-chatbots/tags\">official Tags guide</a> and <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/characters\">Character discovery</a>. implied labels are community ideas, not native tags.</small></p>"
  },
  "image-generation": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"note\">this is the older V3/Qwen image discussion</h2>\n<p>this guide was based on community experiments with V3/Qwen image prompts. that can still be useful, but the official image documentation also describes older diffusion-based tools. i wouldn't assume every prompt trick works in every generator.</p>\n<p>for the practical parts, there are now separate guides for <a href=\"/bots/avatar-images/\">bot Avatars</a> and <a href=\"/bots/conversation-images/\">Conversation Images</a>. use those if you're trying to find a setting or troubleshoot a picture that doesn't look right.</p>\n<p>the notes below are kept as dated community observations rather than a claim about the current model behind every image feature.</p>\n<h2 id=\"prompt\">Qwen prompting is closer to natural language</h2>\n<p>The V3 thread contrasts Qwen with older Stable-Diffusion-style tag/weight prompting. Qwen can work with full sentences, bullet lists and labeled sections.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Subject:\n- adult woman\n- short black hair\n- silver necklace\n\nScene:\n- crowded station hall\n- evening\n- warm overhead light\n\nCamera:\n- side profile\n- low camera angle\n\nStyle:\n- realistic editorial photograph</pre></div>\n<p>The thread repeatedly notes that old weighting syntax such as <code>(word:1.2)</code> is not something to rely on with Qwen. Describe importance with clear language instead.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"formula\">Subject + scene + style is a useful starting formula</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subject:</strong> who/what should be visible and the important physical details.</li>\n<li><strong>Scene:</strong> environment, lighting, weather, time, action.</li>\n<li><strong>Style:</strong> realistic, anime, watercolor, comic, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Add camera/composition as normal language when it matters: “shot from a low side angle,” not only <code>low angle</code>.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"text\">Qwen can sometimes render requested text</h2>\n<p>The thread shows syntax like:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Roadside sign: \"Stop - moose crossing\"</pre></div>\n<p>Text generation is never perfectly reliable, but this is something the newer workflow can attempt more naturally than older tag-based prompts.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"negative\">Negative prompts are unreliable in the V3 discussion</h2>\n<p>Participants repeatedly report that the dedicated negative-prompt field does not behave like Stable Diffusion negative prompting. The thread instead experiments with normal-language “avoid / no / without” wording in the positive prompt.</p>\n<p>This is one of the parts that should be re-tested before this guide is finalized.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"avatar\">The avatar/reference image matters a lot</h2>\n<p>One of the strongest recurring observations is that the avatar anchors subject/style. A blurry or heavily stylized avatar can push generations toward a drawn/stylized output even when the prompt asks for realism.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>use a sharp, clear avatar;</li>\n<li>prefer a consistent style if you want consistent generated images;</li>\n<li>expect anime/cartoon avatars to resist photorealistic style changes;</li>\n<li>the more your prompt fights the avatar, the less consistent the output can become.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"advanced\">Advanced Prompt Settings</h2>\n<p>The thread finds prompt suffixes useful for some repeated details — hair color, clothing, general style hints — but not powerful enough to override everything inferred from the avatar/generated prompt.</p>\n<p>Keep the advanced prompt focused. Several tests report that stacking unrelated style/character instructions can confuse the generator.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"style\">Forcing a style</h2>\n<p>The community experiments with repeating strong style language (“photorealistic photograph,” “bold line work,” etc.) to push the output away from the avatar's default style. It can help, but the thread describes this as a blunt/sledgehammer method and reports mixed results across avatars.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"workflow\">Practical test workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Start with a strong avatar.</li>\n<li>Generate with a simple prompt before adding lots of advanced settings.</li>\n<li>Change one variable at a time: camera, style, outfit, etc.</li>\n<li>If style is wrong, test whether the avatar is the limiting factor.</li>\n<li>Keep good prompt versions instead of rewriting everything after every generation.</li>\n<li>When sharing a result, record the avatar type + prompt/settings so other people can reproduce it.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"compare\">how i'd test V3 prompts now</h2>\n<p>try a short subject + scene + style prompt first, then only change one thing at a time. keep the style and reference picture the same while comparing. a good result from a different generator doesn't prove the same wording works with Qwen.</p>"
  },
  "models-settings": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"intro\">there's no one best model or perfect preset</h2>\n<p>different models work better for different chats. someone might love a certain model for slow-burn RP, and somebody else might hate the way it handles combat or multiple characters. that's normal.</p>\n<p>model names and subscriptions also change, so this page is more about <strong>what the settings do and what to test</strong> than a permanent best-model ranking.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where\">where do you change them?</h2>\n<p>open a chat, use the top-right <strong>⋮ → Generation Settings</strong>, then change the Inference Model or available settings. some advanced controls depend on your plan.</p>\n<h2 id=\"temp\">Temperature</h2>\n<p>this affects how predictable or adventurous the response is. lower can be more focused but sometimes repetitive; higher can be more varied but also less consistent. the official documentation lists <strong>0.7</strong> as the default, with a range of <strong>0–1.5</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"topp\">Top-P</h2>\n<p>Top-P is a <strong>probability-mass cutoff</strong>, not a percentage of the dictionary. the model sorts possible next tokens by their probability, then keeps the smallest group whose probabilities add up to roughly the selected threshold.</p>\n<p>so <strong>0.7</strong> doesn't mean exactly 70% of words are allowed. depending on the next token, that group could be tiny or fairly large. the current SpicyChat docs list 0.7 as the default and 0.01–1 as the range.</p>\n<h2 id=\"topk\">Top-K</h2>\n<p>Top-K keeps at most <strong>K of the most likely next tokens</strong> at that step. for example, Top-K 20 means the model samples from at most 20 candidates for its next token. it doesn't restrict the entire chat to a vocabulary of 20 common words.</p>\n<p>SpicyChat's docs list <strong>90</strong> as the default and <strong>1–100</strong> as the range. the actual result also depends on Temperature, Top-P and the model, so i'd change one setting at a time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"length\">Response Max Tokens</h2>\n<p>the docs list <strong>180 tokens</strong> as the default response cap and up to <strong>300</strong> on supported paid plans. increasing it doesn't force longer replies; if your bot keeps writing walls of text, check your Greeting and style instructions too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"presets\">saving presets</h2>\n<p>SpicyChat's <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/advanced/generation-settings/generation-presets\">Generation Presets</a> page describes up to <strong>200 saved presets</strong> for supported web subscribers. handy if you use one set for short casual chats and another for longer RPG replies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"problems\">what i'd try first</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>First thing to check</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>same wording every reply</td><td>repetitive Greeting or recent replies; then a small Temperature test</td></tr>\n<tr><td>too random or dramatic</td><td>try slightly lower Temperature, with the same opening</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ignoring the character</td><td>contradictory Personality, Scenario or Persona instructions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>cutting off halfway</td><td>response token cap and message length</td></tr>\n<tr><td>very short replies</td><td>Greeting style and the available response cap</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>i'd use the same test scene and change one setting at a time. one lucky or unlucky reply doesn't really tell you much.</p>\n<p>see <a href=\"/bots/repetitive-responses/\">repetition fixes</a> and <a href=\"/bots/writing-style/\">writing style</a> for the parts that aren't just model settings.</p>\n<p><small>checked against <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/advanced/generation-settings\">Generation Settings</a> and <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/advanced/generation-settings/generation-presets\">Presets</a> on November 16, 2026. the live controls and availability can change.</small></p>\n<h2 id=\"nov18-models\">november 18: more context and easier model switching</h2>\n<p>the newest SpicyChat announcement says <strong>Get A Taste gets 6K context</strong> and <strong>True Supporter gets 12K</strong>. the older context chart in the docs hadn't been updated at the time of this check. each model may have a lower maximum, so don't assume every chat gets the full tier amount.</p>\n<p>on <strong>paid plans</strong>, the model picker now puts your most-used models just after your current one. handy if you switch between a couple of favorites a lot.</p>\n<p><strong>GLM 5.3 Flash</strong> is now listed for <strong>True Supporter</strong>. SpicyChat describes it as a lighter GLM option aimed at natural dialogue, playful banter and character-driven adventures without long replies. that's the team's release description, not an independent quality test. the <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/premium-features/ai-models\">AI Models</a> page itself warns the public list may lag, so the in-chat picker is still the best place to check availability.</p>\n<p><small>source: official Discord announcement, November 18, 2026, compared with <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/advanced/tokens-and-context\">Tokens &amp; Context</a> and <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/premium-features/ai-models\">AI Models</a>.</small></p>\n<h2 id=\"josh-model-sampling-nov18\">what Josh's sampling explanation gets right</h2>\n<p>the updated Explaining Tokens thread gives a handy starting picture: <strong>Temperature</strong> affects how strongly the model favors likely options, <strong>Top-P</strong> narrows candidates by combined probability, and <strong>Top-K</strong> limits how many candidates are considered. all of them influence the choice of the <em>next token</em> — not the bot's mood directly.</p>\n<p>lower values aren't automatically accurate, and higher values aren't automatically creative in a useful way. very low sampling can still make mistakes, while high sampling can be coherent. there's no single preset that works best across all bots and all models.</p>\n<p>you can see why a low Top-K might lead to repetitive phrasing, but it's not a guarantee. test with the same opening and a few different responses before deciding whether the setting helps.</p>"
  },
  "creator-analytics": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"what\">What Creator Analytics actually measures</h2>\n<p>SpicyChat's <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/creators/creator-analytics\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Creator Analytics</a> is a <strong>private performance dashboard for your own chatbots</strong>. It helps you tell whether characters are attracting chats, retaining interest and receiving positive feedback. It does not show anyone else's private analytics.</p>\n<p>Open <strong>My Creations → Chatbots → Creator Analytics</strong>, or open one of your bot profiles and select its Analytics/chart action. The default date range is the last 30 days; the dashboard offers 7 Days, 30 Days, 90 Days and Custom.</p>\n<h2 id=\"terms\">Read the numbers correctly</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it counts</th><th>What it doesn't prove</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Total Requests</td><td>Generated replies, including new messages, regenerations and autopilot</td><td>Unique people or completed conversations</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unique Users</td><td>Different people who interacted with your characters</td><td>How long each person stayed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Conversations</td><td>Separate chat sessions started</td><td>All of those chats remained active</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Active Characters</td><td>Your bots that had an interaction in the period</td><td>All characters you have ever published</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Avg Depth</td><td>Average messages per conversation</td><td>The quality of every individual chat</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Avg Rating</td><td>Average user rating (0–100%) plus rating count</td><td>Reliable sentiment from very few votes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Net Likes</td><td>Favorites added minus Favorites removed in the period</td><td>Per-message Like or Dislike reactions</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong> a request is not a new conversation, and a Favorite is not a message reaction. Someone regenerating three replies can add three requests without bringing three new users. Interpret small ratings samples cautiously.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dates\">Date limits and delays</h2>\n<p>The official documentation says Analytics data starts on <strong>January 1, 2026</strong>, while Favorites/Net Likes tracking starts on <strong>February 3, 2026</strong>. Earlier dates cannot be reconstructed from this dashboard. If a selected range includes the pre-tracking period, Likes may show N/A.</p>\n<p>Analytics update on a schedule about <strong>once every 24 hours</strong>, so a brand-new chat, Favorite or rating may not appear immediately. The character list only includes bots that actually had interactions during the selected period. A missing older bot may simply have had zero activity; widen the date range before assuming its data disappeared.</p>\n<h2 id=\"charts\">Trends, your chatbot table and Character Map</h2>\n<p><strong>Daily Trends</strong> lets you switch between Requests, Avg Depth, Likes and Rating. Your <strong>Chatbots</strong> table allows name/tag search, sorting and per-character drill-down; <strong>Export CSV</strong> downloads the table for the chosen date range and sort order. The <strong>Character Map</strong> plots total requests against conversation depth, uses dot size for unique users and color for rating bands.</p>\n<p>For an apples-to-apples comparison, choose the same date range for every character. Compare requests alongside unique users and depth rather than rewarding one giant raw count. Check rating sample size before revising a bot in response to one low score.</p>\n<h2 id=\"practical\">A simple creator review routine</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose a 30-day range and compare the same period month to month.</li>\n<li>Identify bots with many requests but shallow conversations; revisit the greeting and first few replies.</li>\n<li>Identify bots with deeper conversations but few new users; review title, avatar, accurate tags and discoverability.</li>\n<li>Compare rating movement against the number of votes; a tiny sample should not drive a complete rewrite.</li>\n<li>Make <em>one</em> change, record the date, and compare the next comparable period. Avoid guessing that a dashboard change was caused by your edit alone.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For writing changes, continue with <a href=\"/bots/test-your-bot/\">Test Your Bot</a>, <a href=\"/bots/greetings/\">Good Greetings</a> and <a href=\"/bots/tagging/\">Tagging &amp; Discovery</a>. Creator Analytics measures results; it doesn't tell you why a bot behaved a certain way.</p>\n<h2 id=\"leaderboard\">How the monthly Creators Leaderboard differs</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/creators/creators-leaderboard\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">official Creators Leaderboard guide</a> describes two monthly top-25 lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>New Bots:</strong> characters created during the calendar month being ranked.</li>\n<li><strong>All Bots:</strong> characters of any age, including older bots with a steady audience.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The leaderboard is posted early the following month and combines several engagement signals, including messages and unique users, using an <strong>unpublished formula</strong>. Your Creator Analytics totals cannot reproduce its score or predict a rank reliably. It is not simply a competition for raw request count.</p>\n<p>The official guide currently lists rewards for the <strong>top 10 creators in each category</strong>: ranks 1–3 receive one month of I'm All In, and ranks 4–10 receive True Supporter. See the <a href=\"https://spicychat.ai/creators/leaderboard\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">live leaderboard</a> for the current month and recheck the official rules if they change.</p>\n<h2 id=\"privacy\">Privacy and common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Only a creator can view their own Analytics dashboard. Exporting a CSV creates a copy that you control; consider what it reveals before sharing it publicly. Don't interpret a rising request count as proof that a new character setup is better, or a negative Net Likes number as proof that every user disliked the bot.</p>\n<div class=\"guide-callout\"><strong>Source and status:</strong> current feature definitions and leaderboard structure come from SpicyChat's official documentation. The review routine and interpretation tips above are community recommendations, not guaranteed ranking strategies. This is a WIP guide, and dashboard behavior may change.</div>\n"
  },
  "story-continuity": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"durable-vs-temporary\">Identity and current state are different</h2>\n<p>Characters need a recognizable identity, but not every detail is meant to stay true forever. This distinction came up in a community discussion about long chats: a character who initially hates the player might eventually reconcile, or a Persona's everyday outfit might be mistaken for clothes worn every moment. Those are useful <em>design failure modes</em>, not proof of a fixed platform prompt order.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Information</th><th>Where to put it</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Durable character identity</td><td>Personality</td><td>Reserved, stubborn, dry humor; values honesty</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Starting situation</td><td>Scenario and Greeting</td><td>At the first meeting, the character distrusts the user</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Usual Persona identity</td><td>Persona</td><td>Occupation, broad appearance, personal boundaries</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Optional reference facts</td><td>Lorebook</td><td>City history, relatives, named organizations</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Changing values and milestones</td><td>Rulebook</td><td>Trust, inventory, Story clock, Journal events</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What happened during this chat</td><td>Conversation; Memory for older facts</td><td>They reconciled; the promise was fulfilled</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Current temporary state</td><td>Recent narration or supported state meter</td><td>Wearing a borrowed jacket; feeling tired tonight</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>These are <strong>authoring recommendations</strong>, not a claim that each field always has fixed priority in the model's hidden prompt. SpicyChat's <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/creating-chatbots\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">creation guide</a> still supports Personality and advanced Scenario/Example Dialogue.</p>\n<h2 id=\"relationship-changes\">Let a relationship actually change</h2>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Risky permanent card:\n\"She hates {{user}} forever and will never trust them.\"\n\nMore adaptable Personality:\n\"She is slow to trust. Betrayal makes her defensive, but\nmeaningful actions can change her judgment.\"\n\nStarting Scenario:\n\"At the opening scene, she believes {{user}} betrayed her.\"\n\nLater confirmed chat event:\n\"They resolved the misunderstanding and agreed to start over.\"</pre></div>\n<p>If hostility is intended to be permanent, it can remain a core premise. If character development is allowed, do not write an unchangeable verdict into a field that is repeatedly supplied to the model. A reconciled relationship should be reflected in current dialogue and, when it becomes old enough to leave context, an accurate memory or state representation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"clothes-mood\">Avoid freezing outfits, moods and locations</h2>\n<p>A Persona saying \"wears a black three-piece suit\" may cue the model to keep describing that suit, including after sleep or a time skip. Prefer <strong>usually wears</strong> for a general aesthetic, and describe the actual clothing in the current chat. Similarly, \"often reserved\" is more adaptable than \"always angry\"; \"lives in the capital\" is different from \"is currently standing in the capital\".</p>\n<p>Do not invent an automatic clothing change every time a character sleeps. If clothing matters to the scene, establish the new outfit in dialogue. When it does not matter, simply avoid repeatedly emphasizing the old outfit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"context\">Why long chats drift</h2>\n<p>SpicyChat's <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/advanced/tokens-and-context\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tokens &amp; Context guide</a> says the context window holds the character definition, recent messages and instructions; older messages eventually fall out. This can leave a detailed starting description more salient than a relationship change mentioned dozens of messages ago. However, this <strong>does not prove</strong> that older character details automatically overwrite new facts or that Scenario loads first. Those technical claims are not established by the official docs.</p>\n<p>Keep fixed definitions concise. Use memories for confirmed developments once needed; prefer short, clear information over repeating every past exchange. Higher context capacity helps but does not make the model infallible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"memory-now\">Use the rebuilt Memory system accurately</h2>\n<p>As described in SpicyChat's <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/premium-features/memory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">current Memory guide</a>, automatic memories begin <strong>after the conversation exceeds active context</strong>. From then on they are created, updated or removed roughly every <strong>15 back-and-forths</strong>, with one fact per memory, tagged by who it concerns. Only relevant saved memories are retrieved for each reply.</p>\n<p>Before automatic memories begin, <strong>only pinned memories are sent</strong> to the character; an unpinned manual fact can appear under \"Not sent to the chatbot yet\". Pinned and manually added memories are not automatically deleted. Use the message menu's <strong>Save to memories</strong> action for important scenes and edit a stale fact after a retcon. Memory Manager editing and bulk selection are described in the <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/premium-features/memory-manager\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">official guide</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lorebook-rulebook\">Lorebooks and Rulebooks are not interchangeable</h2>\n<p>A Lorebook entry is conditional reference information triggered by recent chat keywords; it is not a persistent calculator or automatically updated history. A Rulebook can track changing quantities, current states or a Journal of configured milestone events. Neither mechanism guarantees that the model will correctly infer an unstated change; use clear movement/event conditions and playtest.</p>\n<p>For your own custom build, consider a modest Trust meter plus a Journal for a few irreversible milestones. The <a href=\"/rulebook/integration/\">system-integration guide</a> explains attachment priority; <a href=\"/rulebook/advanced/\">Advanced Rulebook Design</a> covers tests and conflicts. Don't assume several separately attached Rulebooks combine.</p>\n<h2 id=\"examples-are-not-useless\">Example Dialogue still has a job</h2>\n<p>SpicyChat's <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/creating-chatbots/advanced-settings\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Advanced Settings documentation</a> explicitly describes Example Dialogue as a way to demonstrate a character's voice and reactions. It is optional, consumes tokens and might have little effect if it merely repeats Personality or Greeting, but it is not documented as an ignored field. Test with and without examples using otherwise identical conditions before making strong claims.</p>\n<h2 id=\"continuity-test\">A short reproducible continuity test</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Starting state:</strong> establish initial distrust, clothing and location; record what is intended to change.</li>\n<li><strong>Relationship:</strong> have a clear reconciliatory event, then ask for the character's position without telling it what to say.</li>\n<li><strong>Time skip:</strong> advance to the next morning and check whether yesterday's temporary outfit or mood is incorrectly repeated.</li>\n<li><strong>Context pressure:</strong> continue until early messages are no longer active; inspect Memory Manager and saved milestone state.</li>\n<li><strong>Contradiction:</strong> introduce a correction, then verify stale memories are updated or manually fixed and no static Lorebook entry conflicts.</li>\n<li><strong>A/B comparison:</strong> test the same opening with and without Example Dialogue; keep the model, settings and other fields the same.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"guide-callout\"><strong>Evidence boundary:</strong> the community transcript motivated these scenarios, but its assertions about the exact prompt-loading order, character field precedence and universal uselessness of Example Dialogue are unverified. This guide uses official documentation for platform behavior and labels writing strategies as recommendations.</div>\n<h2 id=\"josh-static-setup-nov18\">when the original description keeps coming back</h2>\n<p>Josh's suit example describes a problem many long roleplays run into. if the Persona permanently says <em>\"{{user}} wears a suit\"</em>, the bot may keep describing that suit after a time skip even though the character changed clothes.</p>\n<p>i'd write <em>\"usually wears formal clothes\"</em> in a reusable Persona, then establish the actual clothes in the scene. if a new outfit or a changed relationship becomes important later, use a clear recent scene description and an accurate memory if needed. no prompt trick makes all models track every clothing change perfectly.</p>\n<p>the thread also gives a rough order for how information enters the model. that's a useful conversation topic, but the official docs don't confirm it as a fixed instruction hierarchy. the safer rule is to <strong>avoid contradictory facts across fields</strong>, not to assume one particular field always wins.</p>"
  },
  "chat-appearance": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"overview\">Native Chat Appearance is available to everyone</h2>\n<p>SpicyChat <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/SpicyChatAI/comments/1xvhn4u/your_chat_your_way_chat_appearance_is_here/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">announced Chat Appearance on November 13, 2026</a>. It is available to <strong>all accounts, including Free</strong>. These settings control how chats are <em>displayed</em>, not what your chatbot's Personality or Scenario says. They apply across <strong>all your chats</strong>, not just the one you have open.</p>\n<p>Open a chat, select the <strong>⋮</strong> menu at the top right and choose <strong>Chat appearance</strong>. The desktop panel has <strong>Colors, Text and Chat</strong> tabs; on mobile the settings open from the bottom, with separate pages. For the source of truth and the exact current controls, see the <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/character-chats/chat-appearance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">official Chat Appearance guide</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"themes\">Themes, colors and speaker identities</h2>\n<p>The six built-in themes are <strong>Default, Night, Classic, High contrast, Soft and Sepia</strong>. You can also choose your own colors for narration (asterisk actions), quoted dialogue, plain text, bold text, backtick highlights, /cmd and OOC text, both message bubbles and the page background.</p>\n<p>Dark and light mode have separate colors. The color picker accepts a hex value and reports readability against its background; the editor can warn about difficult combinations and offer <strong>Fix it</strong>. For multi-character messages, <strong>Speaker colors</strong> assigns colors to names and dialogue on lines starting with a speaker label (for example <code>Keiko: \"Hello\"</code>). Choose Bright, Soft or Earthy, or disable this feature; it does not recolor action narration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fonts\">Fonts and readability</h2>\n<p>The Text tab contains quick choices such as OpenDyslexic, Atkinson, Lexend, Serif and Typewriter, plus a <strong>More fonts</strong> browser of <strong>68 fonts</strong>. You can adjust text size, match the device's font size, line spacing and letter spacing. Try several paragraphs in both dark and light themes before committing to a style; highly decorative fonts may be harder to read during long roleplays.</p>\n<h2 id=\"layouts\">Seven chat layouts and composer controls</h2>\n<p>The Chat tab offers <strong>Chat, Story, Book, Script, Accent, Classic and Minimal</strong> layouts. It also includes bubble opacity, desktop chat width (Narrow through Full width), bot/user avatar sizes or Off, and message-header alignment. These are presentation choices: switching to Book or Script does not change who says what or the underlying chat data.</p>\n<p>Under <strong>While typing</strong>, you can enable <strong>Live formatting</strong> (colored dialogue/actions as you type), <strong>Auto-close marks</strong> for common punctuation and brackets, and <strong>Formatting keys</strong> above the keyboard. These native controls can overlap with third-party composer helpers; test with browser extensions disabled if paired formatting appears twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"commands\">Hiding commands is only visual</h2>\n<p><strong>Show commands &amp; OOC</strong> can be turned off to hide command lines in the displayed chat. According to the official guide, <strong>hidden /cmd and OOC lines are still sent to the character</strong>. This is not a privacy setting, a memory deletion tool or a way to undo prior instructions. It changes visibility, not whether the model can read the command.</p>\n<h2 id=\"styles\">Saving, sharing and importing styles</h2>\n<ol><li>Adjust the appearance; use the <strong>This chat</strong> or <strong>Sample chat</strong> preview and the dark/light preview.</li><li>Select <strong>Done</strong> to save the active changes. Closing the panel without Done discards unsaved changes; Reset only undoes unsaved changes.</li><li>Open <strong>Styles → Save Current Settings</strong>, name the preset (up to 40 characters), and save it. The official limit is <strong>20 saved styles</strong>.</li><li>Use <strong>Share link</strong> to copy a style link. A recipient can preview it and <strong>Apply, Save or Discard</strong> without affecting your original style.</li><li>Use <strong>Import a shared style</strong> in My styles to paste a link. <strong>Text size and line spacing are not included in shared styles</strong>, so users retain their own comfortable reading size.</li></ol>\n<p>Shared style links are about chat appearance, not access to your character cards or conversations. Avoid treating the style as a full settings backup or assuming every accessibility preference transfers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"qol\">Compatibility with browser extensions and custom scripts</h2>\n<p>Some community tools, including DragonScript QoL, can also change chat fonts, layout, color, composer behavior or message presentation. <strong>Compatibility is not guaranteed merely because each setting works alone.</strong> If a control disappears, looks doubled, or a chat layout shifts unexpectedly, temporarily test native Chat Appearance with the extension's overlapping setting disabled.</p>\n<p>For extension developers, a safer design is to <strong>detect the active native appearance mode</strong>, preserve the user's saved native settings, avoid forcing layout styles when the corresponding QoL setting is off, and allow individual optional styling features to be disabled. Prefer scoped CSS and changed-DOM work over repeated full-page rescans. These are engineering recommendations, not official SpicyChat implementation details.</p>\n<h2 id=\"practical-tests\">Quick appearance regression tests</h2>\n<ul><li>Apply a theme, save it with Done, reload the page and open a different chat.</li><li>Test Book and Script layouts with long dialogues, a multi-character response and action text.</li><li>Try Speaker colors with <code>Name:</code> labels and without them.</li><li>Disable Show commands &amp; OOC; confirm you understand that commands are hidden visually but still sent.</li><li>Test Live formatting and Auto-close marks on mobile and desktop, first natively, then with optional extension controls.</li><li>Switch dark/light modes and import a shared style without overwriting text-size accessibility preferences.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"guide-callout\"><strong>Status:</strong> feature names and behavior above are documented by SpicyChat as of November 16, 2026. Extension compatibility notes are community testing advice; they are not a claim that DragonScript or any other extension has already been verified against every layout.</div>\n"
  }
}
);